{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-7","id":"msg_bdrk_r5fvrrpyzp667vfh42kesaquxi4dziy3c5niq3rbocghzp4idiwq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Both `cargo build` and `cargo test` succeed. All tests pass:\n\n- `writenumber_test::test_writenumber` — verified correct output for `writeint` (42, -42, 0, -2147483648), `writeuint` (42, 0), `writehex` (42 in 'x'/'X', 0), and `writepoint` (0x1234, NULL).\n- `writestring_test::write_string` — verified correct output for `writechar` ('A', 'B', 'z') and `writestring` (\"Hello\", \"World!\", \"1234567890\").\n\nSummary of the implementation in `src/ft_printf.rs`, mirroring the C in `c_src/src/ft_printf.c`:\n\n- **`writechar`**: increments `*len`, writes the byte(s) to stdout, returns 1 on success / -1 on error.\n- **`writestring`**: walks the string and calls `writechar` for each char (the C `if (!s) s = \"(null)\"` check is N/A in Rust because `&str` cannot be null).\n- **`writeint`**: handles 0, `i32::MIN` (-2147483648), negative values (writes '-', negates), then digit-buffer + reverse output, exactly like the C version.\n- **`writeuint`**: same digit-buffer pattern over `u64`.\n- **`writehex`**: chooses lowercase or uppercase hex digits based on `'x'` vs `'X'`, handles 0 by emitting '0', mirrors C version.\n- **`writepoint`**: returns \"(nil)\" when LOCATION==2 and the pointer is null, otherwise writes \"0x\" + lowercase-hex representation of the pointer value.\n- **`format`**: dispatches `%c`, `%s`, `%d`/`%i`, `%u`, `%p`, `%x`/`%X`, and `%%`. Uses a thread-local index so the macro's repeated calls walk the args slice in order (since Rust's `Any`-erased args don't behave like `va_list`).\n\nThe remaining diagnostics are pre-existing warnings in `src/lib.rs`/`src/bin/` (the non-snake-case crate name and unused imports in test files), which I left untouched per the instructions."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_c